mode: "100644"
text: "# The Growth Engine\n\nHow TikTok growth actually works in 2026 — the model the rest of this skill builds on. It's\nmeaningfully different from Instagram, so don't carry over IG assumptions.\n\n> The US algorithm is being retrained on US user data through ~mid-2026 (ownership transition), so\n> expect distribution fluctuations — focus on fundamentals (watch time, completion, shares), which\n> carry over regardless. Re-verify specifics quarterly.\n\n## TikTok is an interest graph, not a social graph\n\nThe defining fact: **follower count is not a ranking factor.** TikTok evaluates **every video\nindependently** and tests it on a cold audience. A zero-follower account can hit millions; a big\naccount can flop. **~70–80% of the For You feed is content from accounts the viewer doesn't follow.**\n\nThe consequence: **you don't grow by accumulating followers for reach** (TikTok gives reach to\nnon-followers freely). You grow by **winning the FYP test repeatedly** with content people watch to\nthe end, and by **owning search**. Followers are the *business* asset (LIVE, Shop, owned audience),\nnot the reach mechanism (see `features-and-diagnosis.md`).\n\n## The test-and-expand model (the \"at-bats\" engine)\n\nEvery video runs a lottery in phases:\n\n- **Phase 1 — the test (~100–500 viewers, first 30–90 min).** A mix of your followers and\n  interest-matched non-followers. **Watch-time/completion %** is the key metric here. Most videos\n  live or die in this window — which is why the **first ~1 second** matters more than anything.\n- **Phase 2 — expansion (~1K–50K, progressively less targeted).** Can it hold a broader, colder\n  audience that has no context for you? Most \"good\" videos plateau here.\n- **Phase 3 — breakout (100K+).** Only a small share reach this.\n\nTwo implications: (1) each post is a **cheap at-bat** — more quality at-bats = more chances to hit;\n(2) **videos can resurface days or weeks later** if engagement picks up, so the back-catalog keeps\nworking (TikTok isn't chronological). Note: a warm, **engaged following helps early signals** in the\nPhase-1 window, so retention still matters — but follower *count* itself isn't the lever.\n\n## The signals (2026), strongest first\n\n1. **Completion rate** — #1. The bar has risen: aim for **~70%+ completion** to have a real shot at\n   going wide (up from ~50% in 2024). Shorter, tight videos with high completion beat longer ones\n   that lose people.\n2. **Rewatch / loop rate** — now outranks follower count; one of the strongest distribution signals.\n   Design a loop or a second-watch reward.\n3. **Shares + saves + meaningful comments** — strong \"this triggered action\" signals. Comment\n   *quality* beats count.\n4. **Follows-from-video, profile visits** — sustained-interest signals.\n5. **Likes** — the **weakest** signal. Don't optimize for likes.\n\nMetadata (captions, keywords, hashtags) mostly helps **search** and clustering, not the initial FYP\ntest (see the search pillar in `completion-search-volume.md`).\n\n## The growth loop\n\n```\nconsistent niche content (sustainable at-bats)\n   → Phase-1 test: a 1-sec hook + ~70%+ completion + early shares/comments\n   → Phase-2 expansion: holds a colder audience (rewatch/loop)\n   → breakout: cold viewers watch, rewatch, share\n   → profile visit → binge the back-catalog (Series helps) → follow\n   → fans (LIVE / Shop / community) + an engaged following that warms early signals\nPLUS a parallel evergreen lane: SEARCH (keywords) → discovery independent of the FYP\n```\n\nEvery growth problem is a **specific broken stage or phase** in this loop — diagnose it before\nprescribing (see `features-and-diagnosis.md`). Don't apply random tips.\n\n## Native, not polished\n\nTikTok rewards **TikTok-first, authentic** content over polished ads (audiences prefer a\nless-polished aesthetic; \"make TikToks, not ads\" — see `tiktok-script`). Low-effort content gets\ndetected and buried, so \"volume\" means **consistent quality at-bats**, not spam.\n"
